 ],
 "title": "Functional Programming Strategies in Scala with Cats (draft)",
 "byline": "Noel Welsh, building on Scala with Cats by Dave Pereira-Gurnell and Noel Welsh \u00b7 draft of June 2024, CC BY-SA 4.0",
 "note": "This free draft has since been superseded by the paid, still-in-progress book \u201cFunctional Programming Strategies\u201d; the original Scala with Cats remains free online at scalawithcats.com. Exercise solutions (Part V) are not broken down. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "An expanded rewrite of Scala with Cats that organizes functional programming around reusable \u201cstrategies\u201d as well as Cats type classes, using Scala 3. Part I lays foundations: algebraic data types with structural recursion and corecursion, objects as codata, contextual abstraction with givens and type classes, and reified interpreters. Part II is the classic Cats tour: monoids and semigroups, functors (including contravariant and invariant), monads (Id, Either, Eval, Writer, Reader, State, custom), monad transformers, Semigroupal/Applicative and Parallel, Foldable and Traverse. Part III covers indexed types and interpreter optimization down to stack machines, and Part IV keeps the case studies: testing async code, map-reduce, data validation and CRDTs."
}